    lesson: [
      "After four hundred years of prophetic silence, an angel appears to a teenage girl in a backwater town and tells her she will bear the Son of God. Mary says yes. Joseph, told in a dream, takes her as his wife. They travel for a census, and in the back of a stable in Bethlehem — the city of David, exactly as Micah had foretold (Mic 5:2) — the Word becomes flesh. Angels sing to shepherds. Magi bring gifts from the east. A jealous king kills children. The Saviour starts His life as a refugee in Egypt. The Incarnation is the most disruptive event in human history and the most quiet.",
      "Jesus grows up. At thirty, He is baptized by John in the Jordan, the Spirit descends, the Father speaks, the Trinity is on display. He spends forty days in the wilderness, where the same enemy who deceived Adam tries the second Adam — and loses. Then Jesus begins to preach: 'The time is fulfilled, and the kingdom of God is at hand. Repent and believe the gospel' (Mk 1:15). The kingdom Israel had waited for was not a political restoration but the reign of God Himself, present in the person of Jesus. Where He is, the kingdom is.",
      "His teaching is unlike anything the world had ever heard. The Sermon on the Mount (Matt 5–7) overturns every conventional measure of blessedness, righteousness, and reward. The Pharisees' fastidious obedience is shown to be hollow. The poor in spirit are blessed. Enemies are to be loved. The Father sees in secret. The kingdom belongs to children. Read it slowly and you will find it cuts deeper than any law could.",
      "He does what no prophet had done. He forgives sins (Mk 2). He stills storms (Mk 4). He raises the dead (Lk 7, Jn 11). He cleanses lepers, gives sight to the blind, feeds five thousand from a boy's lunch. He eats with sinners and is criticized for it. He weeps. He prays through the night. He is angry at hypocrisy and tender with the broken. He is both the most attractive and the most demanding person to ever walk the earth.",
      "And He keeps saying things only God can say. 'I am the bread of life.' 'I am the light of the world.' 'I am the resurrection and the life.' 'I and the Father are one.' 'Before Abraham was, I AM.' The Gospels do not present a great teacher who was later turned into a god. They present a man who from the beginning claimed to be God — and asked one question of every person He met: who do you say that I am? That question has not changed.",
    ],
    witnesses: [
      { who: "C.S. Lewis", when: "AD 1952",
        quote: "A man who was merely a man and said the sort of things Jesus said would not be a great moral teacher. He would either be a lunatic — on a level with the man who says he is a poached egg — or else he would be the Devil of Hell.",
        source: "Mere Christianity" },
      { who: "Athanasius", when: "AD 318",
        quote: "He was made man that we might be made God.",
        source: "On the Incarnation", sourceUrl: "https://www.ccel.org/ccel/athanasius/incarnation.html" },
    ],
    crosswalk: [
      { catechism: "Heidelberg", refs: "Lord's Day 14-15 (Q35-39) — the incarnation and ministry of Christ" },
      { catechism: "Westminster Shorter", refs: "Q21-23 — the person and offices of Christ" },
    ],
    reflection: [
      "What does it change to know the King of the universe was born in a stable?",
      "Where in your life does the kingdom of God need to break in?",
      "Read the Beatitudes (Matt 5:3-12) and ask: which one is most foreign to me? Why?",
      "Jesus' question to Peter is His question to you: who do you say that I am? Answer honestly today.",
    ],
    discussion: [
      "Read Luke 2:1-20 aloud. What strikes you about how God chose to enter the world?",
      "Pick one of Jesus' 'I am' statements and discuss what it claims and what it offers.",
      "Discuss Lewis's trilemma. Why are 'great moral teacher' and 'liar/lunatic/Lord' the real options?",
      "What does it look like for our group to live as people of the kingdom in our city this week?",
    ],
    practice: "Read one Gospel straight through this week (Mark is shortest — about ninety minutes). Don't analyze. Just meet Him.",
    journalPrompt: "Write your answer to Jesus' question to Peter: 'Who do you say that I am?' Not what you've been taught. Not what you should say. What do you actually believe about Him today?",
    quiz: [
      { q: "Where was Jesus born, and what prophet predicted it?", options: ["Nazareth, Isaiah", "Bethlehem, Micah", "Jerusalem, Malachi", "Capernaum, Jeremiah"], correctIndex: 1, why: "Bethlehem, the city of David, exactly as Micah 5:2 foretold." },
      { q: "What was the first message Jesus preached?", options: ["Love your enemies", "The kingdom of God is at hand", "Repent of your sins", "Follow me"], correctIndex: 1, why: "Mark 1:15 — the announcement of the kingdom's arrival." },
      { q: "Where does the Sermon on the Mount appear?", options: ["John 14-16", "Matthew 5-7", "Luke 15", "Mark 4"], correctIndex: 1, why: "Matthew 5-7 — the manifesto of Jesus' kingdom." },
      { q: "Which 'I am' statement is NOT in the Gospel of John?", options: ["I am the bread of life", "I am the way, the truth, and the life", "I am the resurrection and the life", "I am the Son of David"], correctIndex: 3, why: "All the great 'I am' statements are in John — Jesus claiming the divine name." },
      { q: "What does Jesus' baptism reveal?", options: ["His humanity only", "The Trinity (Father, Son, Spirit) on display", "His repentance from sin", "His break with John"], correctIndex: 1, why: "Mt 3:16-17 — Father speaks, Spirit descends, Son is baptized." },
      { q: "Why does Lewis say 'great moral teacher' is not an option for Jesus?", options: ["Because He was too radical", "Because of what He claimed about Himself — only God or a madman could say it", "Because He performed miracles", "Because of the resurrection"], correctIndex: 1, why: "Jesus' claims about Himself make 'merely a good teacher' impossible." },
      { q: "What did Jesus' miracles point to?", options: ["His power for its own sake", "Signs that the kingdom of God had broken into the world", "His ability to attract crowds", "His superiority over other teachers"], correctIndex: 1, why: "The miracles are kingdom signs — sickness, death, and chaos giving way to the reign of God." },
      { q: "What is Jesus' question to every generation?", options: ["What will you do for me?", "Are you saved?", "Who do you say that I am?", "Where is your faith?"], correctIndex: 2, why: "Mark 8:29 — the question that hangs over the whole Story." },
    ],
