    lesson: [
      "The bulk of the New Testament — twenty-one of its twenty-seven books — is letters. They were written to actual churches, in actual cities, dealing with actual problems. The letters explain what the Gospels narrated. They take the news of Christ crucified and risen and unpack what it means for how the Church believes, how it lives, how it loves, how it suffers, how it hopes. If the Gospels show us Christ, the letters show us Christ's people figuring out what to do with Him.",
      "Paul wrote thirteen of them. To the Romans, he lays out the gospel with theological care — all have sinned, all may be justified by faith in Christ, all are united with Him in His death and resurrection, all are indwelt by the Spirit, all who are in Christ have no condemnation, all will share in the redemption of creation, all are loved with a love nothing can sever (Rom 8:38-39). To the Corinthians, he addresses messy congregational issues with the same gospel — divisions, sexual immorality, lawsuits, marriage, food sacrificed to idols, gifts of the Spirit, the resurrection. The gospel touches everything.",
      "To the Galatians, he insists fiercely that no Christian must become a Jew first; Christ alone is the door. To the Ephesians, he hymns the cosmic scope of what God has done in Christ — every spiritual blessing, chosen before the foundation of the world, the dividing wall between Jew and Gentile broken, the Church the body of which Christ is the head. To the Philippians, he writes from prison full of joy, urging the same mind that was in Christ — who emptied Himself. To the Colossians, he places Christ above every power and principality. To the Thessalonians, he encourages a young church to endure suffering and live ready for Christ's return. To Timothy and Titus, he gives practical wisdom for shepherding the Church. To Philemon, he asks a slave-owner to receive his runaway slave as a brother — a quiet revolution.",
      "The other letters — Hebrews, James, the two by Peter, the three by John, Jude — bring additional voices. Hebrews shows Christ as superior to every Old Testament figure and institution. James balances Paul's emphasis on faith with insistence that real faith works. Peter strengthens believers for suffering. John insists love is non-negotiable — if you do not love your brother whom you have seen, you cannot love God whom you have not. Jude warns the Church against false teachers. Together they give a rich, multi-voiced witness to the same Jesus.",
      "The letters teach us how to be a church. Read them. They are not optional spiritual extras. They are the practical theology of the Body of Christ. Whatever question you have about Christian living, the letters very probably already addressed it — sometimes more sharply than you would like.",
    ],
